Latin a/A spellings (especially capitals and attached fragments) are positive;
Cyrillic а and я endings are negative; capitalized beginnings weaken this.
Length, nearby letters and diacritics matter, rather than token IDs. Greek
alpha, including its name, is positive. The ordinary English articles a/an
are handled separately. Recent function words and Slavic diacritics soften or reverse
short Latin endings in non-English prose. These are approximate spelling and
language rules, not a lexical lookup or a model of attention.
"""

